	url = {https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105021498453?origin=resultslist},
	abstract = {This article examines continuities and changes in volunteer support for refugees from 2015 to the current situation in Ukraine. It focuses on the question whether motives of volunteers have shifted since their involvement in the 2015 refugee crisis. Moreover, the article examines the extent to which civil society involvement in refugee aid has expanded and whether this engagement is shaped by underlying political motives. It draws on findings from a research project conducted by the International University (IU) in cooperation with a church-based charitable organization in south-west Germany which examines how volunteers themselves perceive and interpret their engagement. The study finds that volunteers are driven by a diverse set of motives. Against prevailing assumptions, the motivations for supporting Ukrainian refugees do not differ significantly from those for supporting refugees from other countries. Similarly, engagement for Ukrainian refugees does not appear to be more politically motivated than that for other refugee groups. Instead, a consistent commitment to address social and political inequities constitutes a key factor throughout all stages of voluntary engagement. © (2025), (Nomos Verlagsgesellschaft mbH und Co KG). All rights reserved.},
