	url = {https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105048237273?origin=resultslist},
	abstract = {Volunteering means giving your time, resources, or skills to help others without expecting anything in return. It is a selfless act meant to make a positive difference for individuals, communities, or society. Volunteering is rooted in kindness, compassion, and the wish to improve others’ lives. People volunteer for various reasons, driven by their own motives, values, and attitudes. According to Ernst & Young’s 2025 report, Generation Z makes up 26% of India’s population. This study highlights the importance of Gen Z, who represent a significant portion of India and the world, in addressing social issues through volunteering, particularly in community work. The research collected data from Gen Z students in Mumbai, aged 18 to 25, who took part in social and community development events. The study employed both qualitative and quantitative methods, including a structured questionnaire administered to 312 participants and in-depth interviews with 20 Gen Z volunteers, to understand what motivates them. It focused on four main motivations: well-being, self-awareness, career and professional growth, and making a positive social impact. The theory of planned behaviour was used to explore the connection between attitudes, motivation, and work performance. NVIVO software helped analyse the responses, and a word cloud was created. Cronbach’s alpha was used to check reliability. KMO and Bartlett’s test confirmed the sample size was suitable. SEM was used to test the hypotheses. Since all indices met the required standards, the model fit well and confirmed strong relationships between Gen Z volunteers’ motivations, attitudes, and performance. © The Author(s), under exclusive license to Springer Nature Switzerland AG 2026.},
