	number = {117},
	pages = {65 - 92},
	doi = {10.7203/CIRIEC-E.117.29480},
	url = {https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105049101025?origin=resultslist},
	abstract = {Volunteering forms a core part of the Third Sector of Social Action (TSAS) and is an important resource for ensuring the operational sustainability of non-governmental organisations (NGOs). However, high staff turnover and the difficulty of establishing stable relationships pose significant management challenges. This theoretical-conceptual article analyses the main perspectives on volunteer motivation to understand the factors influencing retention of volunteers within organisations. To this end, it reviews symbolic, functional and rational approaches, as well as contributions from marketing and Self-Determination Theory regarding perceived value. The analysis reveals that, although the functional perspective has been predominant in identifying motives for volunteering, it is limited in that it does not sufficiently explain the quality of motivation. In contrast, Self-Determination Theory provides a more profound insight into how satisfying basic psychological needs for autonomy, competence and relatedness can foster intrinsic motivation and sustained commitment. Similarly, the concept of perceived value broadens our understanding of volunteering as a meaningful experience by integrating emotional, social, functional, and ethical dimensions. Finally, the study situates these motivations within the context of liquid modernity and generational dynamics, particularly among young people. Implications for managing volunteer relationships are derived from an internal marketing perspective with the aim of strengthening satisfaction, loyalty, and retention. © (2026), (CIRIEC). All right reserved.},
