	doi = {10.1134/S0362119726700489},
	url = {https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105047481007?origin=resultslist},
	abstract = {Abstract: This study examined subjective ratings of physical condition and metabolic responses in six male volunteers aged 38.3 ± 1.8 years who underwent a series of short-radius centrifuge (SRC) runs that generated longitudinal head-to-foot (+Gz) overloads of 2.06 g at foot level. Psychological status was assessed using the short form of the Gießen Subjective Complaints List and the Stress Level Assessment questionnaire. Venous blood samples were collected concurrently with psychological testing for the determination of biochemical markers of the stress response. The results showed that participants tolerated the overloads well and consistently rated their physical condition as good. Serial exposure of male volunteers to SRC-generated +Gz overloads of 2.06 g was accompanied by cardioprotective effects and suppression of gastrointestinal secretory activity. After the series of SRC runs, pain complaints, predominantly in the lower extremities, increased threefold and were attributed to muscular fatigue. The participants exhibited signs of a moderate stress response. In the subjective assessments, this response was reflected primarily in physiological symptoms, a finding supported by correlations between psychological and biochemical stress markers. © Pleiades Publishing, Inc. 2026.},
