	abstract = {This study examines how perceived task significance and citizen gratitude jointly shape public service motivation and turnover intention among rescue volunteers. Drawing on relational job design and public service motivation theory, we analyzed cross-sectional survey data from 572 rescue volunteers nested within 64 nonprofit emergency service organizations in southern Thailand. Latent structural equation modeling with robust estimation was used to test direct, mediated, moderated, and conditional indirect associations. Perceived task significance was positively associated with public service motivation and negatively associated with turnover intention. Citizen gratitude was positively associated with public service motivation, whereas its direct association with turnover intention was only marginally significant. Public service motivation did not significantly predict turnover intention and did not mediate the task significance–turnover intention relationship. However, citizen gratitude significantly moderated the task significance–public service motivation relationship: task significance was strongly associated with public service motivation when citizen gratitude was low, but not when it was high. The study advances a compensatory relational-resources perspective by showing that internal task meaning and external social affirmation may partly substitute for each other in sustaining volunteer motivation. © Authors retain all copyrights.},
