	abstract = {This ethnographic study examines the paradoxical relationship between ideological commitment and organizational sustainability within Chinese volunteer rescue organizations. Drawing on extensive participant observation and in-depth interviews, the paper introduces the concept of public welfare fundamentalism, a deeply embedded ideology that sacralizes unpaid volunteer labor while stigmatizing professionalization, compensation, and strategic communication. The research demonstrates that this ideology functions to construct rescue work as morally pure labor that must remain untainted by market logic, effectively prohibiting essential resource mobilization and concealing the substantial material costs of rescue operations. These ideological constraints produce severe cascading effects involving critical volunteer burnout, chronic resource depletion where members must self-finance equipment, and deteriorating relationships with government partners who increasingly prioritize professionalized services. Ultimately, the study reveals a critical tension where the rigid commitment to preserving the perceived moral purity of volunteer labor undermines the long-term capacity to perform that labor effectively, threatening the very survival of the teams it seeks to elevate. © 2025 The Author(s). Published by Informa UK Limited, trading as Taylor & Francis Group.},
